# How the Bill Splitter works
The Bill Splitter example works out who owes what after a meal:
each person pays for what they ordered, plus their share of the tip.
Change any amount, and every sum on the page follows at once.  Two ideas make that happen:
- the sums are RULES:  worked out from the amounts, never typed in, so they can't fall behind
- the boxes you type in are a FORM bound to the bill:  typing in one changes the bill itself

This guide splits a bill evenly instead, so the whole thing fits on one screen.
Open Bill Splitter, in the Examples, for one line per person.

## Money:  a number written as dollars and cents, e.g. 12.5 => "$12.50"
The same lines as the example's `Money.spellml`.
- `round` drops anything smaller than a cent.
- The last two lines add a 0 when the cents are under ten, so 3.07 doesn't show as $3.7.

<spell>
	to write (amount as a number) as money:
		set cents to round (amount * 100)
		set dollars to (cents / 100) as an integer
		set left over to cents - dollars * 100
		if left over < 10 return "$" + dollars + ".0" + left over
		return "$" + dollars + "." + left over
</spell>

## The bill
What the three boxes hold, each a number with what it starts at:  a $60 dinner for three, with an 18% tip.
Then the sums, each worked out from the lines above, every time one of them changes.

The tip is a percent, `type="percent"`:  Spell keeps 18% as its fraction, 0.18,
so `the amount * the percent` is the tip, with no `/ 100`;  its box, `<input type="percent">`, shows 18.

`<form value={it}>` binds the form to the bill:  each box's `name` is the property it changes.
The sums show as Spell UI's `<statistic>`s:  a big value, its label under it.

<spell-app "bill">
	<property "amount" type="number">60</property>
	<property "percent" type="percent">18%</property>
	<property "people" type="number">3</property>

	<property "tip">the amount * the percent</>
	<property "total">the amount + the tip</>
	<property "each">
		0 if the people < 1
		the total / the people otherwise
	</property>

	<draw>
		<container text>
			<segment>
				<form value={it}>
					<stack>
						<input name="amount" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" label="Bill $" fluid/>
						<input name="percent" type="percent" min="0" label="Tip %" fluid/>
						<input name="people" type="number" min="1" label="People" fluid/>
					</stack>
				</form>
				<divider/>
				<statistics widths="3" size="small">
					<statistic value={write the tip as money} label="Tip"/>
					<statistic value={write the total as money} label="Total"/>
					<statistic value={write the each as money} label="Each" color="green"/>
				</statistics>
			</segment>
		</container>
	</draw>
</spell-app>

## Checks

<spell>
	to test money
		write 12.5 as money should be "$12.50"
		write 3.07 as money should be "$3.07"

	to test splitting
		the total of bill should be 70.8
		write the each of bill as money should be "$23.60"
</spell>

## Try it
- On the right, change People to 4:  Each drops to $17.70 as you type.
- Change the tip to 20:  Tip, Total and Each all follow.
  Nothing in this file says "when the tip changes, redo the total":  the rules are always up to date.
- Add a line of your own, under "each":

  ```spellml
  <property "tip each">the tip / the people</>
  ```

  Then show it on the page, the way "Tip" is shown.

Where next:  Bill Splitter, in the Examples, where each diner is a thing of their own,
with their own row, their own amount and their own share of the tip.
